WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.620
- Hello everybody, welcome.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.970
I'm Alice Kaplan, director of
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:16.603
and professor of French and
Francophone studies at Yale.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.840
Welcome to this webinar,
celebrating the reissue this summer

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.840
of William Gardner Smith's
1963 novel "The Stone Face."

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.890
I'm grateful to the
Whitney, to the Yale Center

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.330
for the Study of Race, Indigeneity,

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.250
and Transnational Migration,
and the Department

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.740
of French and Francophone
Studies for their support.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.900
We'll be looking at a novel that has been

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.140
out of print for so many years

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.680
as issues at its center,
racism, police violence

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.590
and war and memory have taken center stage

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.220
in both France and the U.S.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.520
I'd like to briefly introduce
our three panelists.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.993
Each of them an important
voice on these issues.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.290
Adam Schatz is a contributing
editor at "The London Review

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.187
of Books" and a contributor to
"The New York Times Magazine"

00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:11.200
"The New York Review of
Books" and "The New Yorker."

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.750
He is one of the very keenest analysts

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.120
of Algerian politics and literature

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.290
whether he's writing about
Kamel Daoud's fiction

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.270
or the legacy of French
torture in the Algerian war.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.270
Adam last visited Yale in 2019
to lecture on Franz Fanon.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.427
His 2019 "New Yorker" article,

00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:33.830
"How Does It Feel to be a White Man?"

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.000
William Gardner Smith's Exile,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.180
was important in bringing "The Stone Face"

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.670
to public attention, but
most important of all,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.286
Adam has written the introduction

00:01:43.286 --> 00:01:46.770
to the forthcoming "New
York Review Books" edition

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.277
of "The Stone Face."

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.810
Tyler Stovall is a French
historian whose work

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.088
on the red belt suburbs of Paris,

00:01:55.088 --> 00:01:58.618
on the history of racism
in France, on post-colonial

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:02.950
and transnational history
have defined a field.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.727
I'll mention just two of his many books.

00:02:05.727 --> 00:02:09.600
"Paris Noir: African Americans
in the City of Light"

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.330
where I first learned
about "The Stone Face"

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.980
and his newest, "White
Freedom, the Racial History

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.460
of an Idea" where he
explores the centrality

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.373
of white racial identity to
an understanding of freedom.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.850
He has served as Dean of the
Humanities at UC Santa Cruz

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.580
and as president of the
American Historical Association.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.920
He is currently Dean

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.243
of the Graduate School of
Arts and Sciences at Fordham.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.164
Lia Brozgal is associate professor

00:02:40.164 --> 00:02:42.510
of French and Francophone studies

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.630
in the Department of European Language

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.945
and Transcultural Studies

00:02:45.945 --> 00:02:49.820
at the University of
California Los Angeles.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.680
Her book, "Absent the
Archive: Cultural Traces

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.240
of a Massacre in Paris, 17 October 1961"

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.010
just published by
Liverpool University Press,

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.380
maps through a series of
brilliant cultural analyses.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.720
The afterlife of the October
17 massacres of Algerians

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.120
in Paris, a state crime that
was censored officially,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.720
but that survived through the work

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.730
of artists and writers
like William Gardner Smith.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.194
Lia has lectured at Yale
on the representation

00:03:22.194 --> 00:03:26.680
of October 17 and she was
a much appreciated speaker

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.070
in my war and memory seminar this fall.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.620
So here's how we're structuring the hour.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.960
First, introductory remarks
to set up our conversation.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.840
Adam Schatz will talk about
William Gardner Smith.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.400
Tyler Stovall will discuss Paris Noir

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.090
and the black expatriate literary scene.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.695
Then Lia Brozgal will talk
about the October 17 massacre

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:54.200
and its representation in "The
Stone Face" and other works.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.700
After these introductory
remarks we'll go into dialogue

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:03.100
leaving 10 or 15 minutes at
the end for audience questions.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.460
So please feel free to submit questions

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.097
throughout the hour in the Q and A panel,

00:04:08.097 --> 00:04:11.590
and check the chat where
we'll be posting references

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:15.080
to works mentioned wherever possible.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.283
So Adam, I'll pass the floor to you now.

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:22.500
- Thank you so much, Alice

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.620
for that warm and generous introduction.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.560
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.860
Speaking with Tyler
Stovall and Lia Brozgal,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:36.860
two historians I admire a great deal.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.387
I first became aware of
William Gardner Smith's novel

00:04:43.387 --> 00:04:47.050
"The Stone Face," which
was published in 1963,

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.050
about a decade or so ago
via a friend of mine,

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.280
a scholar at Columbia,
Brent Hayes Edwards,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.850
who has written extensively

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.120
on the literature of the black diaspora.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.210
And then I began to come

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:05.060
upon references to Gardner Smith's work in

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.120
Tyler Stovall's magnificent
history of black expatriates

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.100
in Paris, Paris Noir, and
in the work of Kristin Ross.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.870
And I ordered a copy of the
book because I was intrigued.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.291
And in fact, astonished by the fact

00:05:20.291 --> 00:05:24.640
that the first fictional depiction

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.911
of the October 17th, 1961
massacre of Algerian protesters

00:05:28.911 --> 00:05:31.988
by the Paris police had been written

00:05:31.988 --> 00:05:35.620
by a black American expatriate novelist.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:39.680
In the introduction to this book

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.154
I described Gardner Smith
as a long-forgotten novelist

00:05:43.154 --> 00:05:45.629
but in order to be a forgotten,

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:47.280
you have to have been remembered.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.113
And I don't think,

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:50.680
and Gardner Smith was always
a very obscure figure.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.760
So I became very curious
about who Gardner Smith was.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:59.500
He was born in 1927 in Philadelphia.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.500
He grew up in a poor neighborhood
of black Philadelphia.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.148
And by the time he was in his teens

00:06:08.148 --> 00:06:13.148
he had experienced terrible
beatings by the police.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.850
He became a journalist at
a very young age, at 17.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.030
He was hired by the Pittsburgh Courier,

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.321
one of the most important black newspapers

00:06:23.321 --> 00:06:28.321
in America and not long after, he ended

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.130
up going to Germany with
the American military.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.290
He served there just after the war

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:39.060
for about eight months and on
his return at the age of 22

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.309
he published a striking novel

00:06:41.309 --> 00:06:43.670
called "Last of the Conquerors."

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.480
Was published by Farrar,
Straus and Giroux.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.310
And it's about a black soldier

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:53.787
in Germany who paradoxically finds freedom

00:06:53.787 --> 00:06:57.620
for the first time in the
arms of a white German woman

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.740
in a country that had just
recently slaughtered millions

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.300
of people on racial grounds.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:08.300
And when I read this book,
which is a very lyrical account

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.160
of a romance and also
a devastating treatment

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.640
of racism in the American army,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.640
it struck me that Gardner
Smith was someone who was

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.810
at one in the same time

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.570
very interested in issues
of social justice and racism

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:27.560
and at the same time attracted
to complexity and difficulty.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.659
And one of his great preoccupations

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:33.140
was the question of belonging,

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.420
which is at the center of
his novel of "The Stone Face"

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.548
about a young black American
journalist Simeon Brown

00:07:40.548 --> 00:07:43.410
30 years of age, who comes to Paris.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:48.230
And at first, experiences as
a kind of paradise and falls

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.830
into the soft embrace of
the black expatriate scene

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:56.830
at the Cafe du Tournon
around writers, inspired

00:07:57.991 --> 00:08:01.307
by figures like Richard
Wright and Chester Himes,

00:08:01.307 --> 00:08:05.910
Richard Gibson, but then
Simeon Brown realizes

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:10.450
that in fact, there are
people who are treated much

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:13.550
like black Americans were back home,

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.630
and they were Algerians.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.960
Neither black nor white.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.400
And he's drawn into the
Algerian liberation struggle.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.780
And he arrives in 1960 and a year later

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:31.542
he witnesses the October 17 massacre.

00:08:31.542 --> 00:08:36.542
James Baldwin in his
essay on Richard Wright,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.357
published just after Wright's death,

00:08:38.357 --> 00:08:41.270
"Alas Poor Richard" lamented

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:46.270
that Wright had become
complacent about French racism.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.250
He had begun to enjoy what it was

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.510
like to be a white man in Paris.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:54.280
I think it was somewhat unfair of Baldwin.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.714
In fact, Richard Wright did
write about French racism

00:08:57.714 --> 00:09:02.710
but this, in this essay, Baldwin reflects

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.340
on what he calls the
hazards of expatriation.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.710
He never wrote that novel,
although he did comment

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.560
on the problem in some of his essays.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.820
Gardner Smith is the one
who wrote that novel.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:15.673
And it's, has a very
French theme the same time

00:09:15.673 --> 00:09:18.290
because the theme is fraternity.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.560
What defines brotherhood and what we find

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:25.470
in "The Stone Face" is
that it is not skin color.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:26.760
It is not race.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.770
It is ethical action.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.230
It is solidarity.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:33.910
And in the case of Simeon
Brown, it means disaffiliating

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.910
from other black expatriates
and joining up with Algerians.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.620
It's a rather bitter account
of the black expatriate scene,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.763
not what we're really accustomed to.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.040
So now I'm going to turn the table over,

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.380
turn the floor over to
Tyler Stovall, who is really

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.070
our leading expert on
black expatriates in Paris.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:09:59.163
Thank you very much.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:03.453
- Thank you, Adam.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.480
And thank you Alice for
this wonderful introduction

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:08.930
and for organizing this event

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.650
and thank you to the
Whitney Center at Yale.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.000
I just want to follow up a bit

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.570
on a few of the things Adam said

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.820
about "The Stone Face"
and William Gardner Smith

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.420
and put it into a broader
historical context

00:10:22.996 --> 00:10:25.391
of the whole history of African-Americans

00:10:25.391 --> 00:10:29.270
in Paris in the 20th century.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:33.300
This is a community that, I
mean, had long antecedents

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.108
but really began to take
shape as a defined community

00:10:36.108 --> 00:10:39.533
in the years after the First World War.

00:10:39.533 --> 00:10:43.210
The First World War had
brought the first mass presence

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:45.709
of African-Americans, some 200,000

00:10:45.709 --> 00:10:49.435
to France in the American army.

00:10:49.435 --> 00:10:52.570
And almost all of them
of course, went back home

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.560
but a few stayed, and
African-Americans stayed partly

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.250
because they had introduced jazz to France

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:01.120
and they were able to use
it as a way of surviving,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.380
in fact prospering in the
country during the 1920s,

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.057
during les années folles.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:09.770
And it's when they
first began to develop a

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:11.580
geographical community in the neighborhood

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.620
of Montmartre, in Northern Paris.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.280
So this is a community that, by the 1930s,

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:22.620
and certainly by the 1940s
had a real sense of itself.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.090
And it developed a sense
of itself as blithe,

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:29.540
creating a role in a
front in a foreign nation

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:33.466
that was very hospitable,
very enthusiastic

00:11:33.466 --> 00:11:36.447
about African-American culture in contrast

00:11:36.447 --> 00:11:40.590
to the situation of African-Americans
in the United States.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:44.580
So thus began what I've called
the myth of colorblind Paris.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.370
The idea that Paris was
the city free of racism,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.720
and that in particular, Paris
was a city that welcomed

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:53.100
with open arms African-Americans.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.952
After the Second World War

00:11:54.952 --> 00:11:58.490
this community renewed
itself, it had disappeared

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:00.730
during the war itself.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.690
All the African-Americans
had left virtually

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.650
but many came back after
the Second World War

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:07.521
and they re-installed the community.

00:12:07.521 --> 00:12:11.420
This time it was more of a
literary community headed

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.803
up by Richard Wright, excuse me.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:20.022
And this is the community
that William Gardner Smith

00:12:20.022 --> 00:12:22.933
joined in 1960.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.560
So, and it was a very
fascinating community.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.890
William Gardner Smith
found himself in the heart

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:33.780
of what was in many ways, the
heart of black intelligentsia

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:34.640
and certainly the heart

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.640
of the black literary world
in the 1950s and the 1960s.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:41.777
And yet William Gardner
Smith also represents

00:12:41.777 --> 00:12:45.150
a broader turning point in this,

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.270
the life of this community,
if you will, the sort

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:51.773
of decline of the idea of
France as a colorblind nation

00:12:51.773 --> 00:12:54.480
because if he comes to France at a time

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:59.300
of great welcoming of
African-Americans, he also comes

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:01.840
to France in the middle
of the Algerian War,

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:04.440
France's great anti-colonial struggle.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.750
And it's worth reminding
people that France was involved

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:10.750
in anti-colonial wars from basically 1946

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.540
when it began the war to
repress the Vietnamese struggle

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.450
for independence, until 1962.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.895
So this was a major
theme in French history

00:13:19.895 --> 00:13:21.870
and it is William Gardner Smith

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:26.310
that brings an awareness of
this aspect of French life

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:30.880
which includes an awareness
of French racism to the eyes

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.970
of African-Americans and
to Americans in general.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.490
So he is the one that more

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.241
than anybody else really
calls into question,

00:13:38.241 --> 00:13:42.660
and ultimately destroys, the
myth of colorblind France,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.640
points out that France

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:48.240
in many ways has its own kinds of racism,

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.541
just as much as the United States does,

00:13:50.541 --> 00:13:55.541
and is very explicitly drawn
to an assessment of this.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:56.800
He is also the one that who

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.421
in doing so really draws together
two of the great movements

00:14:01.421 --> 00:14:05.650
for justice of the post-war
world, the movement

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:08.380
for civil rights in the
United States and the movement

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:13.133
for decolonization in
Africa and Asia and Europe.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:17.297
These two movements
have a lot in common and

00:14:17.297 --> 00:14:21.970
"The Stone Face" is one that
makes that commonality explicit

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:24.863
by exploring the ways in
which an African-American

00:14:24.863 --> 00:14:28.850
would see himself drawn
to anti-colonial struggles

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:31.333
and see them as part of his own struggle.

00:14:31.333 --> 00:14:33.047
The classic statement,

00:14:33.047 --> 00:14:35.470
"how does it feel to be a white man?"

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.660
really is very complex
because it basically

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:42.660
is forms William Gardner
Smith's way of saying

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.860
that race is positional.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.770
It is not an absolute,

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:51.070
but race and racism
depend on time and place.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:55.170
And one can be racially
excluded in one context

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:57.530
and very much a part of
the racial superstructure

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.548
and part of the racial
hierarchy in another context.

00:15:00.548 --> 00:15:03.080
And so it's a fascinating novel that looks

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.660
at the ways in which racism is defined

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:09.168
both in the United States and in France.

00:15:09.168 --> 00:15:10.930
And then there's the whole issue

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:15.930
of his portrayal of the October
17th, 1961 demonstration.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:20.170
When I first lived in France,
it was some 40 years ago.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:24.400
And I remember reading
"Libération" on October 17th, 1981

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.757
the 20th anniversary of the demonstration.

00:15:27.757 --> 00:15:31.975
"Libération" had a big
three page spread about it.

00:15:31.975 --> 00:15:34.750
And it was portrayed
on terms that emphasize

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.840
over and over again, how virtually nobody

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.830
in France even knew that
this history existed.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:41.010
And I found that to be true,

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.727
when I asked people I met and
nobody had ever heard of it.

00:15:43.727 --> 00:15:45.860
And some people said Libération was lying

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:48.810
and it did not actually exist.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:53.810
So this demonstration forms
a fascinating plot device

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.370
in "The Stone Face."

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:00.240
It really brings together
William Gardner Smith's arguments

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:03.480
about racism, Algeria,
and the United States

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.630
but it was also of course
a real historical event.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.344
And the fact that it occurred,

00:16:08.344 --> 00:16:10.560
and the fact that he
was able to integrate it

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:13.523
into his novel, showed just how important

00:16:13.523 --> 00:16:16.820
not just this incident,
but the whole relationship

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:20.460
between American racism
against African-Americans,

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:22.340
and French racism against Algerians

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:26.030
between decolonization,
the civil rights struggle,

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:27.630
how important that relationship was

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:30.939
and how determinate it
was of the 20th century,

00:16:30.939 --> 00:16:34.053
mid 20th century, as a whole.

00:16:35.476 --> 00:16:38.337
So I want to now pass the
floor onto a professor

00:16:38.337 --> 00:16:41.520
Lia Brozgal who's going to talk a bit more

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.603
about October 17th.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.990
- Thank you, Tyler,
for that perfect segue.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.480
I came to "The Stone Face"

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.260
through my work on the
cultural representations

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.557
of the October 17th police massacre

00:16:56.557 --> 00:16:59.480
of Algerian demonstrators.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:00.450
So I'm going to make a couple

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.670
of points about how this
episode is represented

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.890
in William Gardner Smith's novel,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.790
but also try to offer
some broader information

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:09.310
about how literature, film,

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:13.050
and other cultural texts have
stepped in to fill the void

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.450
in knowledge surrounding October 17th.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.985
As Tyler just mentioned, even in 1981

00:17:17.985 --> 00:17:21.090
the general discourse in
France regarding this event was

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:24.020
that nobody knew, or nobody had seen,

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:27.250
or that the event itself
had somehow been invisible.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:28.626
Despite the fact that it took place

00:17:28.626 --> 00:17:31.943
in the center of Paris
on a Tuesday evening.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:36.610
So there are three texts that belong

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:37.660
to what I've called the sort

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:41.360
of first wave of cultural
representation of October 17th.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.990
That is three cultural
texts that attempt to

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.900
represent October 17th in
the immediate aftermath

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:48.682
of the heat of the action.

00:17:48.682 --> 00:17:52.654
The first cultural text, if you
will, is a documentary film,

00:17:52.654 --> 00:17:55.437
an hour long documentary film
called (speaking French),

00:17:56.624 --> 00:17:59.610
October in Paris by an amateur filmmaker

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:00.910
named Jacques Panijel.

00:18:01.841 --> 00:18:05.880
The second is a poem
written by Kateb Yasine,

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.030
who is often considered the
doyen of Algerian letters.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:10.480
The poem was written in French

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.900
and it's called in
French, (speaking French)

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.860
which is best translated into English

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.760
as "In the Jaws of the Wolf."

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:18.380
And both of these, both the documentary

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.747
and the poem focus wholly on October 17th.

00:18:21.747 --> 00:18:24.777
"The Stone Face", however,
is the only fictional work

00:18:24.777 --> 00:18:28.247
from this time period of the early 1960s.

00:18:28.247 --> 00:18:29.490
And the premise of the novel

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.835
interesting is not
ostensibly October 17th,

00:18:32.835 --> 00:18:35.570
and indeed within the novel, October 17th,

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:38.190
the massacre that took
place on October 17th, 1961,

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.080
it doesn't really take up that much space.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:44.070
It represents less than 10 pages of prose

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.543
in a novel that's slightly
over 200 pages long.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:48.850
And yet the slenderness of this chapter

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:51.730
I think belies the
significance of October 17th,

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.850
historically for the
protagonist Simeon Brown

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:56.140
of "The Stone Face" and

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:00.940
in terms of the cultural
production of October 17th.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:02.530
So historically I'd just like to point

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.230
out that October 17th,
wasn't just any protest

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.930
and really can't be swept aside

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:08.880
as just another bump
in the turbulent decade

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:10.630
that was the Algerian war.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:12.470
This was really a significant episode

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.840
of state sponsored violence.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:17.080
It stands to this day
as the deadliest episode

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:20.530
of colonial violence to take
place on metropolitan soil.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.080
And it remains the bloodiest day

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.498
in Paris since World War II.

00:19:23.498 --> 00:19:25.044
Now, interestingly, some of our

00:19:25.044 --> 00:19:27.540
some of our listeners
might recall headlines

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:30.917
following the terror attacks
that took place in November

00:19:30.917 --> 00:19:35.917
November, 2015 in Paris, often
the headlines after that day

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.530
consecrated it as the deadliest day

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:39.310
in Paris since World War II.

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:41.040
And in fact, that can only be true

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.060
if you sort of bracket off October 17th.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.290
And this is interesting insofar

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.930
as it really embodies the
ways in which this day and the

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:51.417
the violence of October 17th
has kind of been bracketed

00:19:51.417 --> 00:19:54.709
within the French historical record.

00:19:54.709 --> 00:19:57.390
So the novel, "The Stone Face" comes

00:19:57.390 --> 00:20:00.310
to its dramatic conclusion
with the events of October 17th

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:04.110
making it really the
paroxysm of this novel.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.790
And despite having been
warned not to get involved

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:09.680
with the events, our
protagonist Simeon goes out

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:13.516
into Paris that night, and
he witnesses in central Paris

00:20:13.516 --> 00:20:16.870
firsthand thousands of Algerian protesters

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:19.990
some 20,000 Algerian protestors processing

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:22.970
through iconic Parisian
neighborhoods and boulevards.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:24.390
And then he witnesses the brutality

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:27.020
of the police repression
and indeed the conditions

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:29.507
of detention as Simeon himself steps

00:20:29.507 --> 00:20:32.990
in to try and protect an Algerian woman

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:36.150
who's being beaten by a
Parisian police officer

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:40.090
as she tries to protect her
infant son from the blows.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:43.830
And so Simeon in fact,
experiences October 17th

00:20:43.830 --> 00:20:47.896
as both a witness to the violence and

00:20:47.896 --> 00:20:51.160
in certain ways as a kind of
victim of the violence as well.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:54.350
He's beaten by the police
before he's a summarily thrown

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:57.570
into a paddy wagon and
taken to a holding center,

00:20:57.570 --> 00:20:59.210
a sports stadium that had been converted

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:02.100
into a temporary holding
center for Algerians

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:05.082
and where he spends the night
witnessing the conditions of

00:21:05.082 --> 00:21:09.520
of the Algerians in prison

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:12.960
and then finds himself
released from the stadium,

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.450
due once again to the type of
odd privilege he's experienced

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:20.450
throughout the novel, that is
as being considered privileged

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:23.690
and powerful as compared to the Algerians.

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:25.730
And so in a way, we also see

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.680
that the October 17th is a catalyst

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.410
for his own political thinking,
his own soul searching

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:32.760
the soul searching he's been engaged with

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:34.240
throughout the novel.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.620
Soul searching about race
and racism, his place

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:39.640
in France and his displacement
from the United States.

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:40.473
All of this comes

00:21:40.473 --> 00:21:44.450
to a head with his
experience of October 17th.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:45.290
And he finally comes

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.253
to the conclusion that
it's time to leave Paris.

00:21:49.139 --> 00:21:52.673
I wanted to observe also
that October 17th in,

00:21:52.673 --> 00:21:56.860
within the context of a novel
that is largely realistic

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.878
and plausible, October
17th is the only actual

00:21:59.878 --> 00:22:02.140
historical event that is mentioned

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:04.120
and narrated in great detail.

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.290
Indeed, we really feel
Gardner Smith's background

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.060
as a journalist coming
out in this passage.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:09.480
It's deeply sort of invested

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.910
in a kind of documentary reportage.

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.430
He gives tons of evidence,
including place names

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:18.350
in Paris and, and sites of the violence.

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:20.670
And yet curiously, he gets the date wrong.

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:23.862
We don't know whether this
is intentional or, or a typo

00:22:23.862 --> 00:22:27.444
but in fact, Gardner Smith
writes on October 1st, 1961

00:22:27.444 --> 00:22:29.979
the Algerian National Liberation
Front called on Algerians

00:22:29.979 --> 00:22:31.890
living in Paris to go into the streets

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.310
and hold a demonstration.

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.691
So there's something very curious
about the way in which he,

00:22:35.691 --> 00:22:39.960
we think deliberately,
misrepresents the date.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.470
And we can talk about that perhaps

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:43.710
in the Q and A a little bit.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:44.990
I want to just say a few more things

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.793
about how "The Stone Face" functions

00:22:47.793 --> 00:22:51.270
within the context of representation.

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.640
As Tyler mentioned this
was the very first novel

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:58.420
to narrativize the events of October 17th.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:00.870
Interestingly, it's an American novel.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:05.050
It would take until 1983 to see the first

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:07.940
French novel published that
represent these events.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.253
This is a book called (speaking French),

00:23:10.253 --> 00:23:13.160
or "Murder in Memoriam"
by Didier Daeninckx.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:16.620
And then it would probably take,
in my estimation until 2005

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.700
before we get really this sort

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.810
of broader circulating
work, cultural work to

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:26.160
represent October 17th, then
that would be the film "Caché"

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:29.480
by the Australia Austrian
director, Michael Haneke

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:31.210
which came out in 2005.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:33.230
And while it doesn't represent the events

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:36.399
in terms of, insofar, it doesn't reproduce

00:23:36.399 --> 00:23:39.100
the repression and the massacre,

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:42.050
it does make interesting
sidelong reference to the events

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:44.390
of October 17th in a
way that really embodies

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:48.250
and crystallizes the way the
event itself has sort of lived

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:50.410
in the shadows of French history

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:53.210
and in the shadows of discourse.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:56.240
So "The Stone Face," one of
the most interesting things

00:23:56.240 --> 00:24:00.530
about it, I think, is the
way it so clearly testifies

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.750
to how visible the
violence would have been

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:05.110
in Paris on that Tuesday evening.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:06.810
And indeed how threatening the spectacle

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:09.650
of Algerian political sovereignty

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:14.234
and subjectivity would have
been to the nation at the time.

00:24:14.234 --> 00:24:17.451
It also gives a sense of how discourse

00:24:17.451 --> 00:24:20.660
at the time was absolutely
crackling with race talk.

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:22.410
And this is something you
don't have to rely on the novel

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:24.630
for this, the novel does
a very good job of it,

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:28.410
but we can also confirm this
through contemporary materials

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.920
from the press and through
archival materials.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:32.420
And this is important to underscore

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.980
because it allows us to
construct October 17th

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:37.610
also as a racially motivated episode

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:39.680
of state terror and police violence

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.140
which is extremely important
to ongoing conversations

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.857
about truth and reconciliation in France.

00:24:45.857 --> 00:24:48.249
And it also makes "The
Stone Face" even moreso

00:24:48.249 --> 00:24:52.280
a key text for comparative
transnational study.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:54.360
I just wanted to end on, if I might,

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:57.230
on a quote from the novel towards the very

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:01.210
very end that I think
is incredibly prescient.

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:03.140
And so, as Simeon has announced

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.720
to his French friends that
he's going to be leaving,

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:08.640
one of his friends says to
him, don't go, you'll see.

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:10.140
As soon as the Algerian war is over

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.150
everything in France will be good again.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:14.264
And to this Simeon replies, and I quote,

00:25:14.264 --> 00:25:17.400
"It's a long time before
things will be good here again.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.610
France really hasn't
even begun to suffer yet

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:22.620
from the things that
happened during this war."

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:24.660
And I think in the
context of a France where

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:27.160
we have recently seen
a report commissioned

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:32.160
by the president on how to
effect some kind of change

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:36.620
in Franco-Algeria relationships,
this is the Stora report.

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:39.439
I think we can read Simeon's comment

00:25:39.439 --> 00:25:43.490
as indeed incredibly resonant to this day.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:45.230
And so with that I'll invite Alice

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.910
and the rest of the panelists back

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:50.938
to the forum so that we can exchange about

00:25:50.938 --> 00:25:53.713
this novel and its implications.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:25:57.950
- Thank you so much.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.270
All of you, you're so succinct

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:04.320
and you make me want to
ask so many questions.

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:08.740
I think I'd like to start
with Simeon, with Simeon

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:10.740
with the text, before we move into

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:15.000
the political and ethical issues.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:18.670
First of all, why "The Stone Face."

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:22.230
What about that title
and how does it set up

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.443
the various premises of the novel?

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:30.827
- Well, oh, go ahead Adam.

00:26:30.827 --> 00:26:32.510
- No, no, please.

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:34.020
- No, I was just, I was just going to say

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.540
I think "The Stone Face" is a symbol

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:38.130
of man's inhumanity to man.

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:41.583
I think there's also a
Sartrian in reference there,

00:26:42.664 --> 00:26:44.040
but I'm not a literary scholar,

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.547
so I'm not sure, but
yeah, I mean, I think,

00:26:46.547 --> 00:26:49.320
"The Stone Face," one of
the most arresting parts,

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:50.990
of descriptions of the novel,

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:53.020
and I think, again, something
that's historically based

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:55.570
in the description of October 17th,

00:26:55.570 --> 00:26:58.092
is he he shows a policeman beating up

00:26:58.092 --> 00:27:01.270
a woman and her child, right.

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:04.739
And just the idea that somebody
could do that, shows how

00:27:04.739 --> 00:27:07.310
he, that shows they must
have a face made of stone,

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:08.163
it seems to me.

00:27:09.495 --> 00:27:10.610
- I mean, he's

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:13.140
- He's in fact, working
on painting, he's painting

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:18.250
he's painting "The Stone
Face", which is a reference

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:21.490
to this experience that
he had, being brutalized.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.910
In fact, one of his eyes
was, was gouged out,

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:25.902
there's a patch on his eye,

00:27:25.902 --> 00:27:28.532
he was blind, half blinded by,

00:27:28.532 --> 00:27:31.673
but in a racist attack back in the States.

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:35.310
- It made me think of watching the face

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:40.131
of Chauvin as the verdict
was announced with looking

00:27:40.131 --> 00:27:45.131
at his eyes and trying to
find something in those eyes.

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:48.920
- Right, I mean, it functions

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:50.490
as this kind of recurring leitmotif

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:52.670
in the novel for the face of racism right.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.760
He ascribes it first to the
young boy who, as Adam said

00:27:55.760 --> 00:28:00.121
gouges out his eye,
later to a police officer

00:28:00.121 --> 00:28:05.070
in Philadelphia who beats him senseless.

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.320
And with each of these events

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:11.640
of violent racism, he experiences, he says

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.737
that he sees, again, this
face, it's always the same face

00:28:14.737 --> 00:28:17.210
"The Stone Face", the face of racism.

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:20.030
He sees this again in
the French police officer

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:22.300
who is beating the Algerian woman.

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.080
And as Adam also mentioned, he is

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:25.537
he's an amateur painter in the novel.

00:28:25.537 --> 00:28:29.230
And so he's also trying
to paint "The Stone Face".

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:31.400
And interestingly, one of the, in fact,

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:35.477
the culminating paragraph of
the novel, has him slashing

00:28:35.477 --> 00:28:39.200
up the canvas of the
painting of "The Stone Face"

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:41.620
as he has decided to return
to the United States,

00:28:41.620 --> 00:28:44.580
bought his ticket, booked
passage, and is ready to go.

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.390
So there's a kind of, there's a certain

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:47.720
I hate to say there's a certain triteness

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:50.310
to the way this is handled in the book,

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:53.180
but that's also part of the
way in which he's tried to pull

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:55.480
through this thread of what the face

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:57.940
of racism looks like
and how he reacts to it.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.320
- And that's what's kind of,

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:02.050
both dated and charming about novel.

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:05.044
It is quite a didactic fiction about this

00:29:05.044 --> 00:29:07.950
(speaking French) this
growing awareness that he has

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:11.045
about the stone face, and the notion

00:29:11.045 --> 00:29:14.316
that the stone face is the
same everywhere, which is

00:29:14.316 --> 00:29:17.220
his realization towards
the end of the novel,

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:21.999
was in fact common to
depictions of October 17,

00:29:21.999 --> 00:29:26.999
as Lia knows the, I remember
in the film by Panijel,

00:29:27.177 --> 00:29:32.177
there are references
to the Holocaust in it,

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.499
- [Lia] That's right.

00:29:34.499 --> 00:29:37.550
- Right, and of course
later on, we discover

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:40.978
in the late nineties
and at the time of the

00:29:40.978 --> 00:29:44.730
of the Papon trial, that the Paris prefect

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:47.200
who was presiding over this massacre

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:50.890
was guilty not only of
war crimes in Algeria,

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:52.710
where he served as an official

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:56.406
and also during the Vichy
period of the deportations.

00:29:56.406 --> 00:29:57.239
- That's right.

00:29:57.239 --> 00:29:59.430
- That's the whole
multi-dimensional memory is

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:02.120
a multi-directional memory very much

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:05.001
in operation here, for sure.

00:30:05.001 --> 00:30:08.980
In some ways it's a novel
about how you translate racism,

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:12.341
how he translates the racism of his youth

00:30:12.341 --> 00:30:16.509
in his experience of
Paris and how different,

00:30:16.509 --> 00:30:18.703
and yet how much the same.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:21.460
Can you,
- What's interesting,

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:22.520
- Sorry, Lia, go ahead.

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:23.877
- No, I'm sorry, I was just going to say

00:30:23.877 --> 00:30:24.720
it's just interesting to note that in two

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.740
out of these three cultural texts

00:30:26.740 --> 00:30:30.100
from right after the October 17th massacre

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.380
we have these comparative gestures

00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:35.060
with World War II and the Holocaust

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:38.179
not just in the documentary
is, as Adam mentioned

00:30:38.179 --> 00:30:40.830
(speaking French) but
also in "The Stone Face"

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.290
and so far as Simeon is involved

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.880
with a woman who is a
survivor of the Holocaust

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:48.640
a Polish Jew, who has, whose
parents have been murdered

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:50.500
who herself was a victim
of sexual violence

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.350
at the hands of a Nazi officer.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:54.853
And so again, this idea
that the comparison,

00:30:54.853 --> 00:30:57.560
the comparative gestures, they're not just

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:02.310
in terms of a transnational
look across the Atlantic ocean

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:06.310
but also looking to the Holocaust as well.

00:31:06.310 --> 00:31:08.492
- And there's an incredible scene where

00:31:08.492 --> 00:31:13.160
an Algerian guy's expressing
his feelings of racist rage

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:16.807
about the Jews of Algeria,
who were collaborators

00:31:16.807 --> 00:31:18.450
with the powers and so on.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:22.430
And so no one is really
spared a guilty conscience

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:25.100
in this experience. (laughs)

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:29.550
- And Simeon himself, it
seems has participated

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:32.570
in something that is
described as not quite a rape

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:35.700
but like a rape, when he was a teenager

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:38.113
in a flashback.

00:31:39.784 --> 00:31:41.570
- Yeah, you know, it's interesting
because that whole link

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:43.710
with the Holocaust,
World War II, also exists

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:45.740
in Murder in Memoriam, right?

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:48.940
And in some ways it sort
of flips the script because

00:31:50.236 --> 00:31:52.350
with the Papon trial, it
starts with Papon being

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.490
on trial for his complicity
in the Holocaust.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:56.860
And only then does it
emerge, the whole history

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:59.760
of October 17, in Murder
in Memoriam it's sort of

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:03.640
the reverse, where you first
see the October 17th massacre

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:05.560
and then gradually become aware

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.530
of his participation
in the Holocaust, yeah.

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:10.290
- Yeah, I remember Vergès
was trying to make hay

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:15.290
with that and make France
incapable of judging anybody

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:18.061
because of its own lack
of moral standards.

00:32:18.061 --> 00:32:20.680
- Right.
- Very interesting.

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:23.900
Can we now skip to the end of the novel,

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:25.730
which has been much debated

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:28.080
and there's a very brilliant section.

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:33.050
And Lia talk about, about
the decision by Simeon

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:35.790
which is so different,
Simeon decides to return

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:37.530
to the U.S. and join the movement,

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:41.090
yet William Gardner Smith goes to Ghana

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.730
and this has occasioned a debate including

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:47.560
by Paul Gilroy, who thinks

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:51.300
that returning the United States
is something of a cop-out.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.130
Lia, do you want to take that on?

00:32:53.130 --> 00:32:54.743
It's such an interesting,

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:59.410
- Well, I can say it's, it's fascinating.

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:01.670
I mean, I'm not sure why
characters would have to

00:33:01.670 --> 00:33:04.543
follow the trajectories of
their authors or vice versa.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.220
My understanding though,
is that originally

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:11.770
Gardner Smith had written
the novel's ending

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:16.410
with Simeon staying in France
or perhaps going to Africa

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:20.010
but that it was at the
suggestion of his editor at

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:24.130
Farrar, Straus and
Giroux that he was highly

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:27.433
encouraged to return Simeon
to the United States.

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:30.880
So this might be one of
those instances where

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.320
the editorial hand weighed
heavier than what the author's

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:36.348
original desires or
intentions might have been.

00:33:36.348 --> 00:33:39.240
But indeed Paul Gilroy has
criticized this ending.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:40.950
He calls it a kind of capitulation

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:42.743
to narrow a demands of kinship.

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:45.680
And it's sort of interesting to see how

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:47.890
the trajectories of Simeon Brown

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:50.723
and William Gardner Smith are
then cleaved apart, right?

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:53.010
William Gardner Smith goes to Ghana

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:55.929
for a time, where he lives and is involved

00:33:55.929 --> 00:34:00.470
with liberation struggles
and then eventually returns

00:34:00.470 --> 00:34:01.550
to France where he lives

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:03.943
out the rest of his
unfortunately short life.

00:34:05.953 --> 00:34:07.520
I think it's a little bit of a shame

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:10.210
to judge the return of
Simeon Brown so harshly.

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:11.930
I'm not sure exactly that it

00:34:11.930 --> 00:34:12.990
capitulates to anything.

00:34:12.990 --> 00:34:15.260
On the one hand, it separates Simeon Brown

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:18.980
from his compatriots,
the other black Americans

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:20.830
in exile in Paris, but it returns him

00:34:21.785 --> 00:34:24.293
to his homeland to
participate in a struggle.

00:34:25.380 --> 00:34:27.530
And I think William, it seems
that William Gardner Smith

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:29.430
what we know from his correspondence is

00:34:29.430 --> 00:34:33.130
that the decision to stay,
the decision not to go back

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:35.863
to the United States, was
something that haunted him.

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:39.120
He wrote in letters to
his mother, for example,

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:41.580
that although he felt
comfortable in France

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:44.960
he also felt uncomfortable
because he couldn't be political.

00:34:44.960 --> 00:34:46.720
He couldn't engage.

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:47.990
He was constantly reminded

00:34:47.990 --> 00:34:49.710
that it wasn't his place to engage

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.814
in the political struggles in France.

00:34:52.814 --> 00:34:56.240
And this does seem to have
been something that weighed

00:34:56.240 --> 00:34:57.810
on his conscience quite a bit.

00:34:57.810 --> 00:34:59.010
We also know that several

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:00.770
of his other works were
translated into French.

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:01.603
And the understanding is

00:35:01.603 --> 00:35:03.970
that he never tried to have
"The Stone Face" translated

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:06.070
into French because he knew he was dealing

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:07.560
with a kind of political dynamite

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:09.710
and that it would just
get him into trouble.

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:12.940
- I just realized that
that was partly his desire,

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:16.770
because we've all wondered
why it hasn't been translated.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:20.520
It's now in translation
at the (speaking French)

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:22.890
it's coming out in October,

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:26.410
- That's great news.
- next October.

00:35:26.410 --> 00:35:29.030
So it will finally exist
in the French context.

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.663
It'll be very interesting
to follow the reviews.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:33.940
- Absolutely.

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:37.190
- Yeah, I mean, one of the
things that interests me

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:41.050
about this question, Alice,
is that in some ways, the idea

00:35:41.050 --> 00:35:44.640
that Simeon would return to
the United States is the most

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:48.080
fitting end to the novel,
the way it takes shape.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.653
I mean, if you see the
novels as a repudiation

00:35:50.653 --> 00:35:55.653
of the politics of exile
and an emphasis on the unity

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:59.050
of world struggles, well,
why shouldn't he go back

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:00.500
to the United States?

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:02.240
You know, why shouldn't he
go back to a place where

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:04.280
he can be politically active?

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:06.160
But the point is, as you
say that, first of all, he,

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:08.660
William Gardner Smith, the
author did not do that.

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:11.680
Many others did not do that as well.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:14.100
I mean, all you have to do
is look at James Baldwin

00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:16.610
who was one of the biggest critics

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:18.420
of people like Richard Wright.

00:36:18.420 --> 00:36:21.240
Well, James Baldwin spent most of the rest

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:24.029
of his life in the French Riviera, right.

00:36:24.029 --> 00:36:26.310
He went back to America from
time to time to give talks

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:28.248
and so on and so forth,
but he never went back

00:36:28.248 --> 00:36:31.770
to become politically involved
permanently in America.

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:34.790
So, you know, it does say something about

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:38.950
the attraction of this
kind of experience, even

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:41.800
for those people that in many
ways reject it politically.

00:36:42.646 --> 00:36:43.632
- Adam said something, yeah,

00:36:43.632 --> 00:36:46.520
- He did, I mean, of course
a Baldwin did go back

00:36:46.520 --> 00:36:48.240
at a critical juncture.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:52.540
He went back in 1957 and
he writes about that sense

00:36:52.540 --> 00:36:56.390
of survivor's guilt and
he felt moved to return,

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:58.612
particularly in "No Name in the Street."

00:36:58.612 --> 00:37:02.454
I think Gilroy's essay is brilliant, but

00:37:02.454 --> 00:37:07.454
I don't see how returning to
the States to become involved

00:37:07.579 --> 00:37:12.574
in civil rights movement 19,
you know, in the early 1960s

00:37:12.574 --> 00:37:17.574
in 1963, in 1961, this novel
could have been a capitulation.

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:21.610
And in fact, in this
novel, he says the reason

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:26.610
he's returning is to join
forces with America's Algerians.

00:37:28.673 --> 00:37:32.330
The experience of exile
has nourished his thinking

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:35.910
and has allowed him to place
the black American experience

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:40.020
in a wider context of
anti-colonial liberation struggles,

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:41.540
but has widened his perspective.

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:44.590
And he feels the same desire,

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:48.360
I think, that leads his friend Akhmed,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:53.360
a young Kabyle medical
student, to leave France

00:37:55.060 --> 00:37:58.430
and to fight in the in
the mountains of Kabylia

00:37:58.430 --> 00:38:02.260
with the FLN, so I think it is consistent.

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:04.767
At the same time, we do know

00:38:04.767 --> 00:38:09.767
that originally Simeon Brown
decides to go to Africa.

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:12.950
In fact, there are
conversations in the novel

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.738
with African students,
none of whom is named,

00:38:15.738 --> 00:38:19.740
why don't you come build an
independent African state

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:20.830
with us, you're needed there.

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:22.830
You'll be comfortable among your people.

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:25.646
- That's the kinship.

00:38:25.646 --> 00:38:28.470
- But he doesn't go to Africa, of course.

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:33.040
I think that was in fact, a
request of his editor at FSG.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:34.420
I don't see it as a capitulation.

00:38:34.420 --> 00:38:37.410
I see, because he's going
under a very different,

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:40.810
a very different pretext, and
he's not simply going home.

00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:42.613
He sees home in a different light.

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:44.903
- And not to belabor the point

00:38:44.903 --> 00:38:46.900
but narratively it's
totally coherent, right?

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:49.630
I mean, we see Simeon
Brown sort of beginning to

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:53.310
imagine himself involved in
sort of analogous struggles.

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:54.473
I'm just going to quote,

00:38:54.473 --> 00:38:56.700
"he imagined himself on an
Algerian mountain fighting

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:58.410
alongside Akhmed, then he imagined himself

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:00.300
in Angola fighting with the nationalists,

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:01.280
or in the Congo aiding

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:03.460
the imprisoned prime
minister Patrice Lumumba

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.580
Then he returned with ache to reality."

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:07.822
And there's also on the
micro level, the setup of the

00:39:07.822 --> 00:39:10.280
the little girl in Arkansas

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:13.380
who he sees being prohibited
from going into the school.

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:15.750
And then the children he
sees, the Algerian children

00:39:15.750 --> 00:39:18.340
he sees in the streets with
their mothers being threatened

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:20.810
by police and so on sort of
the micro narrative level.

00:39:20.810 --> 00:39:23.095
And in terms of all the
parallelisms that have set up

00:39:23.095 --> 00:39:27.340
it's coherent that that
Simeon would make this move.

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:30.040
- Yeah, you seem to be
implying that he actually

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:31.770
does learn something from this

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.090
transnational experience of racism.

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:37.844
It brings to mind a
passage that we've talked

00:39:37.844 --> 00:39:42.171
about where Simeon is, I guess he's

00:39:42.171 --> 00:39:44.787
on a bus and it's thinking of Harlem.

00:39:44.787 --> 00:39:47.273
He's in Paris, and he thinks of Harlem.

00:39:48.169 --> 00:39:50.740
We actually think, Sarah,
do you have the passage

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:52.513
so that you can share the screen?

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:58.110
Great, Adam, do you feel like reading?

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:02.940
- Sure, I can. "Northward toward Harlem.

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:04.690
The further north the bus moved,

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:07.090
the more drab became the
buildings, the streets

00:40:07.090 --> 00:40:08.650
and the people, cheap stores

00:40:08.650 --> 00:40:11.920
selling clothes, furniture,
kitchen utensils,

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.290
easy terms 10 months to pay.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:16.750
Cafes became dimmer, the streets, narrower

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:20.410
and noisier, more and more
children filled the sidewalks.

00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:23.780
Men out of work with nothing
to do and no place to go, stood

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:27.270
in sullen, futile groups
on street corners.

00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:29.550
Arab music blared from the dark cafes

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:31.990
or from the open windows of bleak hotels.

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:34.010
Then suddenly, police were everywhere,

00:40:34.010 --> 00:40:37.230
stalking the streets, eyes
moving insolently from face

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:40.970
to face, submachine guns
strung from their shoulders.

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:42.910
It was like Harlem, Simeon thought,

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:44.920
except that there were
fewer cops in Harlem

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:47.370
but maybe that too would come one day.

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:50.070
Like Harlem and like all
the ghettos of the world.

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:52.960
The men saw through the window
of the bus had whiter skins

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:54.730
and less frizzly hair, but they were

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:57.610
in other ways like the
Negroes in the United States.

00:40:57.610 --> 00:41:00.745
They adopted the same
poses, stashing on corners

00:41:00.745 --> 00:41:05.550
ready for and scared of
the ever possible trouble,

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:07.830
eyes solid and distrusting, dressed

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:11.780
in pegged pants, flashy shirts,
and narrow pointed shoes.

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:14.800
He could almost hear them
saying, whachu putting down, man,

00:41:14.800 --> 00:41:16.740
just playing it cool,
just playing it cool, man,

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:19.070
trying to keep ole Charlie off my back.

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:22.650
Ole Charlie paced the street,
waving his submachine gun.

00:41:22.650 --> 00:41:25.034
Street vendors shouted
their wares in Arabic,

00:41:25.034 --> 00:41:27.710
fruit, clothes, vegetables.

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:30.560
He remembered the pushcarts in
his childhood on 10th street

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:32.840
the sweating men plugging
holes in watermelons

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:35.710
so you could taste them,
opening fish and cleaning them

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:38.280
and scaling them for you,
shouting in the mornings,

00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:40.800
any old rags, any papers, any iron?

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:43.580
The odors of rotting food and
cooking mingled in the air

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:45.680
and he remembered how
they had smelled to him,

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:47.140
the fried chicken or the greens,

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:50.040
the uncollected garbage
and the alleys and gutters.

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:52.860
Arab music assailed them from all sides.

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:55.480
The Blues, where was the Blues Singer now?

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:57.990
In the dismal cafes, men played pinball

00:41:57.990 --> 00:42:00.066
or football machines or stood at counters

00:42:00.066 --> 00:42:03.600
staring at nothing, empty
cups in front of them.

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:04.560
There were no women.

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:07.347
The police paced the
streets, their faces hard."

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:09.893
- Thank you so much.

00:42:10.952 --> 00:42:12.270
So in the hard faces

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:14.870
there's the leitmotif of
"The Stone Face", right?

00:42:14.870 --> 00:42:17.420
It's one of the most written passages

00:42:17.420 --> 00:42:21.070
I think in a novel that
uses dialogue, I think often

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:25.790
as a kind of paddle boat to
get somewhere in an argument.

00:42:25.790 --> 00:42:30.790
I mean, it's very, very
literary and layered.

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:38.973
- And after that passage, he writes

00:42:38.973 --> 00:42:43.973
that Simeon expected to
be seen as a brother.

00:42:44.735 --> 00:42:47.814
But in fact, he's not, he's an outsider

00:42:47.814 --> 00:42:52.180
and he's suspect, not just
because he's not Algerian

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:54.253
but also because he's American too.

00:42:57.084 --> 00:43:00.150
- He has described this novel

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:03.163
as the novel of the guilty bystander.

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:07.500
You agree?

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:09.927
- Well, I was gonna mention the, one

00:43:09.927 --> 00:43:14.370
of the other famous
passages in the book where

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:16.250
Simeon is walking past
a group of Algerians

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:17.083
and someone yells out,

00:43:17.083 --> 00:43:20.090
"hey, how does it feel to be a white man?"

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:22.550
Of course, no Algerian in
Paris, would ever have said

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:25.700
that to an African American
in the 1960s, right?

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:27.210
This is not an Algerian speaking.

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:29.523
This is Simeon speaking,
conscience speaking.

00:43:29.523 --> 00:43:30.800
(panelists laughing)

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:35.560
So, you know, in other words,
all of the freedom in Paris

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:39.110
that he's been luxuriating in,
is really not freedom at all.

00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:40.710
It's really white privilege.

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:42.380
Okay, he doesn't use those words

00:43:42.380 --> 00:43:44.330
but that that's clearly what it is.

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:45.980
And it's one of the first novels

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:48.550
I know that makes that point

00:43:48.550 --> 00:43:50.793
so clearly in an international context.

00:43:51.860 --> 00:43:53.870
- Wow, I mean, why don't we,

00:43:53.870 --> 00:43:56.900
we're gonna switch to the
audience questions soon

00:43:56.900 --> 00:43:59.560
but I want to ask you to end,

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:02.730
what does this novel
have to tell us today?

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.743
How does it fit into the current debate?

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:11.460
I think you addressed
some of those issues, Adam

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:16.460
in your preface about the current trouble

00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:20.899
over identity politics
versus universalism.

00:44:20.899 --> 00:44:23.523
What position does this novel take?

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:26.220
- Well, I, on the one hand

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:28.070
it's very much an anti-racist novel.

00:44:28.070 --> 00:44:31.270
And on the other hand, it's
a rather subtle critique

00:44:31.270 --> 00:44:34.760
of a politics based narrowly on identity.

00:44:34.760 --> 00:44:38.220
As Tyler pointed out earlier,

00:44:38.220 --> 00:44:43.220
even the notion of race is situational.

00:44:43.696 --> 00:44:48.430
He is not black in the way
that he is black in America

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:50.003
once he is in Paris,

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:55.870
it's the Algerians who
occupy that position.

00:44:55.870 --> 00:44:57.690
And he becomes aware, of course

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:02.094
that his black brothers so to speak,

00:45:02.094 --> 00:45:05.178
the writers, the Cafe du Tournon circle,

00:45:05.178 --> 00:45:08.800
have become quite complacent

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:12.379
about the privilege that
they are enjoying in Paris

00:45:12.379 --> 00:45:16.750
and would prefer not to be associated

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:18.970
with this despised minority.

00:45:18.970 --> 00:45:23.340
So in order to move into solidarity

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:27.750
with the Algerians, he has
to disaffiliate from people

00:45:27.750 --> 00:45:30.540
around the Cafe du Tournon,
the black expatriates.

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:35.540
So to me, it's very much
a novel about the grounds,

00:45:36.070 --> 00:45:41.070
the ethical grounds of
fraternity and solidarity,

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:44.011
and a critique of the notion

00:45:44.011 --> 00:45:48.510
that race is a sufficient grounds

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:50.093
for that kind of solidarity.

00:45:51.650 --> 00:45:55.530
It also places race in a much
more international context,

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:59.110
in a context of, in this
case of decolonization.

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:01.287
So in that sense, I think it's

00:46:01.287 --> 00:46:03.587
refreshingly heterodox.

00:46:05.302 --> 00:46:08.180
It cuts against, I think some of

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:13.180
the rigid, and essentialist thinking about

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:19.400
about identity that has
become, unfortunately,

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:24.400
I think a reflex in the
States in recent years.

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:29.340
- Yeah, Lia, I'm thinking
about all the work you've done

00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:34.340
on the invisibility and
visibility, the 17th of October

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:37.740
and the way that he makes it visible,

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:39.966
versus the way that state violence

00:46:39.966 --> 00:46:43.090
and police violence is visible today

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:45.393
and how that has changed.

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:50.582
- Oh, well, I mean, of
course I think, you know

00:46:50.582 --> 00:46:55.582
one of the obvious things
is technology there.

00:46:55.750 --> 00:46:58.350
We have this, we have
this fictionalized account

00:46:59.465 --> 00:47:02.930
of William Gardner Smith in
the voice of Simeon Brown,

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:06.450
but obviously William Gardner
Smith witnessed what happened

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:10.240
on October 17th and
recounted it in this novel.

00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:13.810
But we do know, and we
have now the evidence

00:47:13.810 --> 00:47:15.800
that photographs were taken that night.

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:18.010
There's photographic documentary evidence

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:20.450
of the police violence.

00:47:20.450 --> 00:47:22.670
If it had happened today,
we would have body cams.

00:47:22.670 --> 00:47:24.940
We would have instant release
of this kind of footage.

00:47:24.940 --> 00:47:26.290
These things would become memes,

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:28.530
they would be posted on social media.

00:47:28.530 --> 00:47:32.310
Clearly in 1961, that's not an option.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:34.540
And indeed the photographs that were taken

00:47:34.540 --> 00:47:36.565
by journalists circulating
in Paris that night

00:47:36.565 --> 00:47:39.008
were censored by the government.

00:47:39.008 --> 00:47:40.830
So while some of them made their way

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:43.480
into the press before the
press was fully censored

00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:44.630
most of them weren't seen

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:47.745
until decades after the events themselves.

00:47:47.745 --> 00:47:50.584
So there's a certain interesting
way in which comparing

00:47:50.584 --> 00:47:53.870
with today, you know,
technology makes things visible

00:47:53.870 --> 00:47:56.380
in a way that it couldn't have in 1961.

00:47:56.380 --> 00:48:00.215
- Despite that huge banner
across the Seine saying

00:48:00.215 --> 00:48:03.000
they drown Algerians.
- Here they drown Algerians.

00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:04.770
That's also a fantastic story

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:09.100
because numerous accusatory
graffiti were painted

00:48:09.100 --> 00:48:12.110
along the Seine the
weeks after October 17th.

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:13.850
And yet they were, as soon
as they were discovered

00:48:13.850 --> 00:48:15.060
they were guarded by the police,

00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:17.770
so that people couldn't take
pictures of them ostensibly.

00:48:17.770 --> 00:48:21.270
And we have one photograph
of one of these graffiti

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:23.180
that was taken by an amateur photographer

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:25.080
who happened to crop out,

00:48:25.080 --> 00:48:27.250
when he took the photograph,
he happened to be able

00:48:27.250 --> 00:48:31.230
to crop out the gendarmes
who were guarding the tag.

00:48:31.230 --> 00:48:33.240
It's just an extraordinary
document that also

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:35.869
didn't see the light of
day until I think 1986

00:48:35.869 --> 00:48:38.945
when Humanité published
its 25th anniversary

00:48:38.945 --> 00:48:42.353
special coverage of October 17.

00:48:44.206 --> 00:48:47.450
- I want to make another
point about October 17th

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:50.500
which at times is not addressed

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:53.580
but I'm going back to my
history, my own history

00:48:53.580 --> 00:48:56.620
as a historian of the Paris
suburbs, because in many ways

00:48:56.620 --> 00:49:00.670
October 17th is an invasion
of the city by the suburbs.

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:02.010
Of Algerian communities

00:49:02.010 --> 00:49:04.570
and other communities living outside Paris

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:05.720
coming in and invading the heart

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:07.620
of the city and being destroyed precisely

00:49:07.620 --> 00:49:10.260
because they are at a place
where they do not belong.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:12.055
And this is all happening at a time when

00:49:12.055 --> 00:49:17.055
both the independence of
Algeria and the reorganization

00:49:17.068 --> 00:49:19.810
of the greater Paris area are
happening at the same time,

00:49:19.810 --> 00:49:23.240
so in fact, the three new
departments that are created

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:25.410
outside Paris are given numbers

00:49:25.410 --> 00:49:27.760
from the three former Algerian departments

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:30.831
once Algeria becomes independent, right?

00:49:30.831 --> 00:49:33.277
So there's a sense of
wishing, you know, the

00:49:33.277 --> 00:49:36.670
the Paris suburbs are the
new post-colonial France.

00:49:36.670 --> 00:49:37.940
- Exactly, yeah, yeah.

00:49:37.940 --> 00:49:41.440
- And you know and just to,
I want to just piggyback

00:49:41.440 --> 00:49:45.888
on that point, Tyler, there
is a a major demonstration

00:49:45.888 --> 00:49:49.860
that takes place in February of 1962,

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:51.567
but the demonstrators are,

00:49:51.567 --> 00:49:53.380
- White.
- white French people

00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:57.459
in the communist party
orbit at the Metro Charonne.

00:49:57.459 --> 00:50:00.386
8 demonstrators are killed.

00:50:00.386 --> 00:50:02.380
And that's what gets remembered.

00:50:02.380 --> 00:50:05.560
It's the Charonne massacre,
it's not October 17.

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:07.101
- Right, right.

00:50:07.101 --> 00:50:10.299
- It's interesting,
- Go head Lia.

00:50:10.299 --> 00:50:11.910
- It's interesting just to
piggyback off both of those

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:14.829
if I might, it's true
that proportionally, most

00:50:14.829 --> 00:50:16.890
of the Algerian demonstrators that came

00:50:16.890 --> 00:50:19.080
into Paris that night
lived outside of Paris,

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:20.990
lived in the suburbs, lived in the

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:23.096
large slum that was Nanterre.

00:50:23.096 --> 00:50:25.450
But significant numbers also lived

00:50:25.450 --> 00:50:28.120
within the city limits,
but were invisible.

00:50:28.120 --> 00:50:30.260
So the event is often
figured, when it was figured

00:50:30.260 --> 00:50:32.470
in the press as a kind of,
invasion of the Capitol, right.

00:50:32.470 --> 00:50:35.540
A taking of the capital from
forces coming from without,

00:50:35.540 --> 00:50:38.210
Ignoring, in fact that
large numbers of Algerians

00:50:38.210 --> 00:50:40.721
lived (speaking French),
within the city limits,

00:50:40.721 --> 00:50:43.940
but weren't seen to the
public until they emerged

00:50:43.940 --> 00:50:46.580
in this kind of spectacular
demonstration on October 17th.

00:50:46.580 --> 00:50:48.237
- I mean, people that lived
in the (speaking French)

00:50:48.237 --> 00:50:49.747
for example, on the top floors.

00:50:49.747 --> 00:50:51.080
- Right, right, right.

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:53.117
Or, you know, five men to a bed.

00:50:53.117 --> 00:50:55.760
- Now back in the days when Paris actually

00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:58.817
had mixed classes because of them, but.

00:50:58.817 --> 00:51:00.840
- Right, Right.
- Exactly.

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.010
- Listen we've got some
interesting questions

00:51:03.010 --> 00:51:05.710
and a little bit of time.

00:51:05.710 --> 00:51:09.150
Was Gardner Smith
influenced by Frantz Fanon

00:51:09.150 --> 00:51:11.747
who like you, connected
as thinking about racism

00:51:11.747 --> 00:51:15.110
and the black condition
to anti-colonial struggle?

00:51:15.110 --> 00:51:17.720
And in particular, the
Algerian war of independence.

00:51:17.720 --> 00:51:19.630
- You know it's interesting.

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:23.997
You will not find, even
in his work of reportage,

00:51:23.997 --> 00:51:27.030
"Return to Black America"
which was published in,

00:51:27.030 --> 00:51:29.482
I think 1970, and it's
a quite interesting book

00:51:29.482 --> 00:51:32.890
a single reference to Fanon.

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:34.450
There's no reference to Fanon.

00:51:34.450 --> 00:51:36.640
He, I mean, he might've been aware of,

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:39.733
he was certainly aware of
"The Wretched of the Earth"

00:51:40.818 --> 00:51:43.587
and perhaps also "Black
Skin, White Masks."

00:51:43.587 --> 00:51:45.010
"The Wretched of the Earth" was published

00:51:45.010 --> 00:51:50.010
in December of 1961,
this novel appears in 63.

00:51:51.060 --> 00:51:52.473
So likely he knew it.

00:51:53.590 --> 00:51:55.144
There is a chapter in the book

00:51:55.144 --> 00:51:58.510
where he meets two young Algerian women

00:51:58.510 --> 00:52:00.541
who've been tortured back home.

00:52:00.541 --> 00:52:05.541
And one of the women says
that because we've contributed

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:10.840
to the Algerian struggle, we
can now choose our own husbands

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:12.820
and we will never go
back to wearing the veil.

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:14.640
Now, of course, this is
a very, it turns out to

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:17.650
be a very optimistic understanding
of what's going to happen

00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:19.370
in post independence Algeria,

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:24.370
but it's an account that exactly
mirrors Fanon's description

00:52:25.050 --> 00:52:28.410
of Algerian women in year five
of the Algerian revolution

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:31.700
or a dying colonialism,
the essay Algeria Unveiled.

00:52:31.700 --> 00:52:35.358
So my sense is that he probably
was familiar with Fanon

00:52:35.358 --> 00:52:38.880
but there are no traces,
no explicit traces,

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:39.963
of Fanon in his work.

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:42.750
- Fanon didn't have a presence in Paris

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:44.600
in those years, right?

00:52:44.600 --> 00:52:45.433
I mean, he was,

00:52:45.433 --> 00:52:49.130
- No, no, when he left,
he left Paris and, sorry,

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:53.730
he left Leon in late 1953, returned very

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:56.860
very briefly on his way to Tunis

00:52:56.860 --> 00:52:59.320
in late 1956 and never came back.

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:03.903
So no, but François Maspero,
the left wing publisher,

00:53:03.903 --> 00:53:08.903
published (speaking French)
and then (speaking French).

00:53:09.310 --> 00:53:13.239
So he did, he had a small
presence in an activist community

00:53:13.239 --> 00:53:15.174
and people who were part of the

00:53:15.174 --> 00:53:18.070
(speaking French) circle
the people who were

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:19.400
in the (speaking French) network

00:53:19.400 --> 00:53:21.380
were aiding the Algerian rebels.

00:53:21.380 --> 00:53:22.410
They knew about his work.

00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:24.060
And in fact, there is a character

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:27.920
in "The Stone Face" named Henri,
a Frenchman who is the one

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:31.210
Frenchman who admits that
there is a problem with racism.

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:33.310
By the end of the novel, he's working

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:36.513
with the (speaking French),
the (speaking French) network.

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:43.670
- We have a question about
how much William Gardner's

00:53:43.950 --> 00:53:48.490
life corresponded to the
life of Simeon in the novel.

00:53:48.490 --> 00:53:51.810
I mean, things, I imagined the
question could involve things

00:53:51.810 --> 00:53:53.900
like the relationship with Maria,

00:53:53.900 --> 00:53:56.540
with a Jewish Holocaust survivor, or

00:53:58.539 --> 00:54:02.239
- I think that, well,
there is a certain parallel

00:54:02.239 --> 00:54:03.630
obviously, because he was a journalist,

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:05.560
he had been at the Pittsburgh Courier

00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:07.553
he ended up working with AFP.

00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:12.090
I don't know that he painted,
there's some indication

00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:15.660
if you read Hazel Rowley's
biography of Richard Wright

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:20.330
that Maria, the Polish
Jewish survivor, is based on

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:23.210
an Ashkenazi Jewish woman
who had been a lover

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:25.530
of Richard Wright, and Gardner Smith ended

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:28.088
up kind of stealing her
from Richard Wright.

00:54:28.088 --> 00:54:30.107
But I don't know,

00:54:30.107 --> 00:54:34.040
but otherwise their
stories very much diverge.

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:38.783
Gardner Smith was married three times.

00:54:39.812 --> 00:54:44.812
I think the parallels are
rather superficial in the end.

00:54:45.970 --> 00:54:49.230
- Yeah, here's an interesting question,

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:51.933
bringing in yet another identity group.

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:57.950
Was Smith in any way aware of
the Pied-Noir born in Algeria?

00:54:57.950 --> 00:54:59.343
For example, Camus.

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:05.810
Estrangement was part of the
identity of the Pied-Noir.

00:55:05.810 --> 00:55:08.480
Do you think that estrangement
can be a concept linked

00:55:08.480 --> 00:55:11.613
with a kind of feeling
that goes on with racism?

00:55:12.945 --> 00:55:14.530
Complex question.

00:55:14.530 --> 00:55:18.730
- Well the Pied-Noir were
not Pied-Noir in 1971,

00:55:18.730 --> 00:55:21.167
they only became Pied-Noir
when they were quote unquote,

00:55:21.167 --> 00:55:22.850
"repatriated" to France.

00:55:22.850 --> 00:55:24.803
That's when the term emerges.

00:55:28.483 --> 00:55:31.530
There is a reference
toward the end of the novel

00:55:31.530 --> 00:55:35.970
to French soldiers
returning home and to groups

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:38.690
like the OAS forming, which
were composed obviously,

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:42.620
comprised obviously of ultra Pied-Noirs.

00:55:42.620 --> 00:55:46.160
But I don't think that he really,

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:48.375
certainly he would have
read Camus, no one,

00:55:48.375 --> 00:55:51.280
no writer was unfamiliar
with Camus' works.

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:54.160
But I think the politics,
the political vision

00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:56.420
that's espoused here
has very little to do,

00:55:56.420 --> 00:56:00.163
and in fact is almost
antithetical to Camus' vision.

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:04.170
- Here's another complicated question.

00:56:04.170 --> 00:56:07.320
Why would the protagonist
want to exchange one place

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:09.300
of racism for another?

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:11.943
It seems to me that the American
experience can be no place

00:56:11.943 --> 00:56:15.290
of exchange, especially
given the legal racism

00:56:15.290 --> 00:56:17.883
that he would have met upon his return.

00:56:19.110 --> 00:56:24.110
- Well, let me respond to that,
with a bit of an anecdote.

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:26.870
One of the people I interviewed
for my book "Paris Noir"

00:56:26.870 --> 00:56:29.690
was a woman who had been,
she was a black woman

00:56:29.690 --> 00:56:33.120
who been a student in
Paris in the early 1960s.

00:56:33.120 --> 00:56:36.020
And she talked about how black students

00:56:36.020 --> 00:56:37.730
from America at that
time would come to Paris

00:56:37.730 --> 00:56:40.380
and meet with Richard Wright
and meet with all these people.

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:42.820
But she said there was a
difference because rather

00:56:42.820 --> 00:56:44.980
than having the idea that they
was going to sit at the feet

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:47.550
of Richard Wright and learn
wisdom and create literature

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:50.670
and all that, they would come
and challenge him and say

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:52.640
why aren't you back in the United States?

00:56:52.640 --> 00:56:53.820
What are you doing here?

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:56.839
Why aren't you a part of the
struggle basically, right?

00:56:56.839 --> 00:57:00.820
And so I think this novel
sort of reflects that, that

00:57:00.820 --> 00:57:05.250
in effect, people had a duty
to be a part of that struggle.

00:57:05.250 --> 00:57:08.027
You know, this is 1961, 1962

00:57:08.027 --> 00:57:10.930
the freedom rides are
taking place in the South.

00:57:10.930 --> 00:57:12.200
For example, there's all sorts

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:13.520
of violent incidents going on.

00:57:13.520 --> 00:57:16.190
People are literally putting
their lives on the line.

00:57:16.190 --> 00:57:17.680
And there's this idea that,

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:19.987
you do not have the right to, sit here

00:57:19.987 --> 00:57:24.017
in Paris in comfort and talk
about how terrible things are.

00:57:25.340 --> 00:57:29.010
- I mean, even Angela
Davis, it's very clear she's

00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:31.520
in Frankfurt studying with Adorno.

00:57:31.520 --> 00:57:33.730
It's very clear she's eager to get back.

00:57:33.730 --> 00:57:36.170
That's a little later, right?

00:57:36.170 --> 00:57:38.550
Feel the same urge.

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:40.500
Here's a great question.

00:57:40.500 --> 00:57:42.740
Which could be our last one.

00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:46.090
So what, what do you guys
think are the textual

00:57:46.090 --> 00:57:50.100
and political stakes of
this forthcoming translation

00:57:50.100 --> 00:57:53.240
of "The Stone Face" into
French, thinking, for instance

00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:56.360
of how it would interact
with the Stora report

00:57:56.360 --> 00:57:59.839
which mentions the role of
re-additions and translations,

00:57:59.839 --> 00:58:02.950
and the current discussions
of race in France,

00:58:02.950 --> 00:58:07.060
which tend to dismiss
intersectionality and positionality

00:58:07.060 --> 00:58:11.053
as symptoms of the Americanization
of French public debate.

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:15.072
And we have one minute. (laughs)

00:58:15.072 --> 00:58:16.610
- Macron would say, it's
the very first example

00:58:16.610 --> 00:58:19.343
of woke culture from America
invading France, right.

00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:23.333
- Except I can't think
of a less woke book.

00:58:23.333 --> 00:58:24.573
- Not woke, yeah.

00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:26.830
- I think it's going to go,

00:58:26.830 --> 00:58:28.677
I mean, I hope it goes off like something

00:58:28.677 --> 00:58:31.580
of a bomb, with no victims.

00:58:31.580 --> 00:58:34.810
Obviously it's an important book.

00:58:34.810 --> 00:58:37.233
I think it's going to be disturbing.

00:58:40.100 --> 00:58:42.240
I think, going back to something Adam said

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:45.900
the question of how
anti-essentialist this book is,

00:58:45.900 --> 00:58:48.110
that's one of the big
lessons to take away.

00:58:48.110 --> 00:58:53.110
And I would hope somehow that
would also inform the debate

00:58:53.670 --> 00:58:54.850
because I would hate to see it reduced

00:58:54.850 --> 00:58:56.520
to a kind of a centralist debate such

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:58.853
as what do Americans
know about, et cetera.

00:59:00.190 --> 00:59:02.880
But I think, to answer John's question

00:59:02.880 --> 00:59:04.090
I think the stakes are high.

00:59:04.090 --> 00:59:05.720
I hope it will have the visibility.

00:59:05.720 --> 00:59:07.280
I hope the translation
will get the visibility

00:59:07.280 --> 00:59:10.223
in France for the stakes to play out.

00:59:11.060 --> 00:59:12.100
As I mentioned, I think

00:59:12.100 --> 00:59:14.440
in the context of the (speaking French)

00:59:14.440 --> 00:59:16.170
it's very interesting to have this account

00:59:16.170 --> 00:59:17.870
come to light in France right now.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:21.342
- Invitation to write a critical essay

00:59:21.342 --> 00:59:23.974
in French on the book.

00:59:23.974 --> 00:59:24.990
(panelists laughing)

00:59:24.990 --> 00:59:26.760
That's a wonderful place to stop.

00:59:26.760 --> 00:59:30.710
I thank you so much for being here

00:59:30.710 --> 00:59:34.350
and for lending your
expertise to the celebration.

00:59:34.350 --> 00:59:36.400
- Thank you so much, Alice.
- Thank you.

